# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Two things learned the hard way, both worth keeping in mind before editing:
#
#  1. Pages *joins* the values of a header set by more than one matching rule. Two
#     overlapping Cache-Control rules therefore produce a malformed value and the
#     platform default silently wins. One Cache-Control rule per path.
#  2. Pages applies its own 4-hour Cache-Control to recognised asset extensions
#     (.css, .js, .png, .svg) and that max-age beats anything set here — only the
#     extra directives merge in. Which is why site.css and site.js carry a content
#     hash in their filename (see scripts/build.mjs): a 4-hour cache is only safe
#     on a URL that changes when the file does.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()

# Hashed CSS/JS, the brand marks, and the icon set. The 4-hour platform TTL is
# fine for the former; must-revalidate keeps the rest from being served stale
# past it.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Fonts are the one thing worth caching hard, and .woff2 is not in the list of
# extensions Pages overrides — so this rule is the one that really sticks.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
